window.CURRICULUM_6 = [{"id":"patterns6","index":1,"name":"Patterns & Number Play","subtitle":"Number sequences, shape patterns, and playful puzzles","emoji":"🧩","palette":{"from":"#a855f7","to":"#6d28d9"},"lessons":[{"id":"pn1","title":"Number & Shape Patterns","icon":"🔢","kind":"lesson","story":"Mathematics is, in large part, the search for patterns — and for the reasons why those patterns exist, all around us in nature, art and numbers.","teach":[{"title":"Number sequences","body":"Among the most basic patterns in mathematics are number sequences: counting numbers, odd numbers, even numbers, triangular numbers (1, 3, 6, 10, 15...), square numbers (1, 4, 9, 16, 25...), and cube numbers (1, 8, 27, 64...). Each sequence has its own rule for what comes next.","visual":"🔢"},{"title":"Pictures reveal patterns","body":"Visualising sequences with pictures can reveal beautiful relationships. For example, adding up consecutive odd numbers (1, 1+3, 1+3+5, 1+3+5+7...) always gives you the square numbers — a fact you can prove just by rearranging dots into a square grid!","visual":"🟦"},{"title":"Shapes have patterns too","body":"Shape sequences are patterns too. In the sequence of Regular Polygons — triangle, square, pentagon, hexagon and so on — the number of sides follows the counting numbers starting at 3. The branch of mathematics that studies shape patterns is called geometry.","visual":"🔺"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The sequence 1, 3, 6, 10, 15, 21, ... where each number can be arranged as dots in a triangle, is called the ______ numbers.","options":["triangular","square","cube","prime"],"answer":0,"explain":"Each number in this sequence can be arranged as a triangular grid of dots."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Adding up consecutive odd numbers starting from 1 (1, 1+3, 1+3+5, 1+3+5+7...) always gives you the ______ numbers.","options":["square","triangular","cube","even"],"answer":0,"explain":"This can be shown by rearranging dots into a perfect square grid."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The sequence of Regular Polygons — triangle, square, pentagon, hexagon... — has a number of sides that matches the counting numbers starting at ______.","options":["3","1","0","10"],"answer":0,"explain":"A triangle (3 sides) is the first regular polygon in the sequence."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"1, 4, 9, 16, 25, ... are called square numbers. What is the next number in this sequence after 25?","answer":36,"explain":"36 = 6 × 6, continuing the pattern of square numbers."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"1, 8, 27, 64, 125, ... — numbers that can be arranged as dots filling a cube — are called ______ numbers.","options":["cube","square","triangular","even"],"answer":0,"explain":"Each of these numbers equals a whole number multiplied by itself three times."}]},{"id":"pn2","title":"Digits & Puzzles","icon":"🧩","kind":"lesson","story":"Numbers can be playful too! From special 'supercells' to Kaprekar's magical constant, let's explore the hidden puzzles inside ordinary digits.","teach":[{"title":"Supercells","body":"A cell in a number grid is called a 'supercell' if the number in it is greater than all its neighbouring cells (the cells immediately next to it). Finding and creating supercells is a fun way to explore how numbers compare to their surroundings.","visual":"🔲"},{"title":"Palindromes and digit sums","body":"A number that reads the same from left to right and right to left, like 66, 848 or 1111, is called a palindrome. Meanwhile, the digit sum of a number — adding up all its digits — can reveal surprising patterns, like the fact that 68, 176 and 545 all share the same digit sum of 14!","visual":"🔁"},{"title":"Kaprekar's magic number","body":"D.R. Kaprekar, a mathematics teacher from Devlali, Maharashtra, discovered that if you take any 4-digit number with at least two different digits, arrange its digits to form the largest and smallest possible numbers, and subtract repeatedly, you will always reach the number 6174 — now called the Kaprekar constant!","visual":"✨"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A cell in a number grid is called a 'supercell' if the number in it is ______ than all its neighbouring cells.","options":["greater","smaller","equal","unrelated"],"answer":0,"explain":"This is the defining rule for spotting a supercell."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A number that reads the same from left to right and right to left, like 1441 or 787, is called a ______.","options":["palindrome","supercell","factor","multiple"],"answer":0,"explain":"Palindromic numbers mirror themselves around their centre."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"D.R. Kaprekar discovered that repeatedly arranging a 4-digit number's digits to form the largest and smallest numbers and subtracting always leads to which constant?","options":["6174","1000","9999","1234"],"answer":0,"explain":"This surprising number is now called the Kaprekar constant."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"The digits of the number 68 add up to a digit sum of ___.","answer":14,"explain":"6 + 8 = 14, the same digit sum as 176 or 545."}]},{"id":"pn-boss","title":"Pattern Master","icon":"🏆","kind":"boss","boss":{"name":"Pattern Master","taunt":"Numbers and shapes both hide their secrets in patterns. Show me you can spot them all!"},"story":"A final test across everything in this world — number sequences, shape sequences, supercells, palindromes and Kaprekar's magic number.","teach":[],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which sequence do you get when you add up pairs of consecutive triangular numbers, like 1+3, 3+6, 6+10?","options":["Square numbers","Cube numbers","Prime numbers","Odd numbers"],"answer":0,"explain":"1+3=4, 3+6=9, 6+10=16 — all square numbers!"},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these numbers is a palindrome?","options":["7337","1234","5678","9012"],"answer":0,"explain":"7337 reads the same forwards and backwards."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The supercell rule states that a cell is coloured if the number in it is ______.","options":["greater than all its neighbouring cells","smaller than all other cells","the average of the whole table","always zero"],"answer":0,"explain":"Supercells stand out by being bigger than everything touching them."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which branch of mathematics studies patterns in shapes, like the sequence of Regular Polygons?","options":["Geometry","Number theory","Algebra","Statistics"],"answer":0,"explain":"Geometry is the study of shapes, while number theory studies patterns in numbers."}]}]},{"id":"geometry6","index":2,"name":"Lines, Angles & Shapes","subtitle":"Points, lines, rays, angles in degrees, and circles","emoji":"📐","palette":{"from":"#0ea5e9","to":"#0369a1"},"lessons":[{"id":"la1","title":"Points, Lines & Rays","icon":"📍","kind":"lesson","story":"A pencil's sharp tip, a fold's crease, a beam from a lighthouse — geometry's most basic ideas are hiding in ordinary things all around us.","teach":[{"title":"The basic building blocks","body":"A point marks a precise location with no size. A line segment is the shortest path between two points, with two endpoints. A line extends forever in both directions. A ray starts at one point and goes on forever in just one direction.","visual":"📏"},{"title":"Naming them","body":"A line segment from A to B is written AB. An angle is formed by two rays sharing a common starting point, called the vertex — for example, ∠DBE, where B is the vertex.","visual":"📐"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these extends forever in BOTH directions?","options":["A line","A line segment","A ray","A point"],"answer":0,"explain":"A line has no endpoints and extends endlessly in both directions."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these starts at one point and goes on forever in only ONE direction?","options":["A ray","A line","A line segment","A point"],"answer":0,"explain":"A ray has a starting point but no ending point — like a beam of light."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The shortest path between two points, with two endpoints, is called a…","options":["Line segment","Ray","Line","Angle"],"answer":0,"explain":"A line segment is bounded at both ends by its two endpoints."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In an angle, the point where the two rays meet is called the…","options":["Vertex","Arm","Endpoint","Segment"],"answer":0,"explain":"The vertex is the shared starting point of the two rays (arms) that form the angle."}]},{"id":"la2","title":"Measuring Angles in Degrees","icon":"📐","kind":"lesson","story":"The size of an angle is really just the amount of turn between two rays — and mathematicians long ago agreed to measure that turn using a unit called the degree.","teach":[{"title":"Degrees","body":"A full turn is divided into 360 equal parts, each called a degree (1°). A full turn = 360°. A straight angle (half turn) = 180°. A right angle (a quarter turn) = 90°.","visual":"🔄"},{"title":"Classifying angles","body":"Acute angles are less than 90°. Right angles are exactly 90°. Obtuse angles are between 90° and 180°. Straight angles are exactly 180°. Reflex angles are between 180° and 360°.","visual":"📊"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"How many degrees are in a full turn?","answer":360,"explain":"A full turn is divided into 360 degrees."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A straight angle is half of a full turn. How many degrees is that?","answer":180,"explain":"Half of 360° is 180°."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"An angle that measures exactly 90° is called a…","options":["Right angle","Acute angle","Obtuse angle","Reflex angle"],"answer":0,"explain":"90° — a quarter turn — is the definition of a right angle."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"An angle measuring 200° is called a…","options":["Reflex angle","Acute angle","Right angle","Straight angle"],"answer":0,"explain":"200° is between 180° and 360°, which is the range for reflex angles."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Two lines that meet at a right angle are called…","options":["Perpendicular lines","Parallel lines","Intersecting curves","Diagonal lines"],"answer":0,"explain":"Lines meeting at exactly 90° are called perpendicular."}]},{"id":"la3","title":"Circles & Constructions","icon":"🧭","kind":"lesson","story":"Arm yourself with a compass — fixing its tip at one point and rotating the pencil traces out a perfect circle, and reveals what makes a circle a circle.","teach":[{"title":"Drawing a circle","body":"Fixing the tip of a compass at a point and rotating the pencil around it traces a circle. The fixed point is called the centre, and the distance from the centre to any point on the circle is called the radius.","visual":"⭕"},{"title":"Every point, the same distance","body":"Every single point on a circle is exactly the same distance — the radius — from the centre. That's the property that makes a circle a circle, and not some other curve.","visual":"📏"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In a circle, the fixed point around which the compass rotates is called the…","options":["Centre","Radius","Vertex","Diameter"],"answer":0,"explain":"The centre is the fixed point the compass tip stays anchored to."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The distance from the centre of a circle to any point on the circle is called the…","options":["Radius","Perimeter","Angle","Vertex"],"answer":0,"explain":"The radius is the fixed distance between the centre and the circle's edge."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What tool is used to draw a perfect circle?","options":["A compass","A protractor","A ruler only","A set square"],"answer":0,"explain":"A compass keeps one point fixed while the pencil traces a constant distance around it."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"If every point on a curve is the same distance from one fixed centre point, the curve must be a…","options":["Circle","Square","Triangle","Line"],"answer":0,"explain":"Equal distance from a centre point is the defining property of a circle."}]},{"id":"la4","title":"Squares & Rectangles","icon":"▭","kind":"lesson","story":"A carpenter checking a doorframe, a tailor squaring off a cloth panel — knowing exactly what makes a rectangle a rectangle (and a square a special rectangle) is one of geometry's most useful everyday skills.","teach":[{"title":"What makes a rectangle a rectangle","body":"A rectangle ABCD satisfies two properties: R1) its opposite sides (AB & CD, and AD & BC) are equal in length, and R2) all four angles are exactly 90°. A square satisfies its own two properties: S1) all four sides are equal in length, and S2) all four angles are 90° — so a square is really just a special rectangle where every side happens to be the same length.","visual":"▭"},{"title":"Rotating doesn't change the shape","body":"If you rotate a square or rectangle on paper, its side lengths and angles stay exactly the same — so a rotated square is still a square, and a rotated rectangle is still a rectangle. Only the orientation changes, never the properties.","visual":"🔄"},{"title":"Constructing a square with ruler and compass","body":"To construct square PQRS with 6 cm sides: draw PQ = 6 cm, construct a perpendicular to PQ at P, then use the compass to mark S on that perpendicular so PS = 6 cm. Construct a perpendicular to PQ at Q too, and mark R so QR = 6 cm. Joining S to R completes the square.","visual":"🧭"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which property is shared by BOTH squares and rectangles?","options":["All four angles are 90°","All four sides are equal","Only two sides are equal","No two sides are ever equal"],"answer":0,"explain":"R2 and S2 both require every angle to be a right angle — that's true for every rectangle, squares included."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A square is best described as —","options":["A rectangle where all four sides happen to be equal in length","A shape with no right angles","A rectangle with only two equal sides","A completely different shape from a rectangle"],"answer":0,"explain":"A square satisfies every rectangle property, plus the extra condition that all sides are equal."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"If a square piece of paper is rotated on the table, is it still a square?","options":["Yes — rotating doesn't change its side lengths or angles","No — rotation destroys the shape","Only if it's rotated by exactly 90°","Only if it's rotated less than 45°"],"answer":0,"explain":"Rotation only changes orientation, not the lengths or angles that define the shape."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"To construct a square PQRS with side length 6 cm, you draw PQ = 6 cm, build a perpendicular at P, then mark S on it so that PS = ___ cm.","answer":6,"explain":"Every side of a square is equal, so PS must also measure 6 cm."}]},{"id":"la5","title":"Diagonals & Equidistant Points","icon":"📏","kind":"lesson","story":"Join the opposite corners of a rectangle and you get its diagonals — and a compass can find you a point that's the exact same distance from two other points, without any trial and error.","teach":[{"title":"Diagonals of a rectangle","body":"Joining opposite corners of a rectangle PQRS gives its two diagonals, PR and QS. In every rectangle, the two diagonals are always equal in length — you can predict this before even measuring.","visual":"✖️"},{"title":"A diagonal splits each angle it touches","body":"A diagonal divides each of the two right angles it passes through into two smaller angles. In a general rectangle these two smaller angles are different sizes — but in the special case of a square (where all sides are equal), a diagonal splits each angle into two EQUAL halves.","visual":"◧"},{"title":"Finding a point equidistant from two points","body":"To find a point that is an exact fixed distance from two given points — like locating the peak of a triangular roof 5 cm from both walls — open your compass to that radius and draw an arc from each point. The spot where the two arcs cross is exactly that distance from both original points, with no ruler trial-and-error needed.","visual":"🏠"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In any rectangle, how do the lengths of its two diagonals compare?","options":["They are always equal in length","They are always different lengths","One is always exactly double the other","Diagonals cannot be measured"],"answer":0,"explain":"This is true for every rectangle, no matter its side lengths — including squares."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In which special rectangle does a diagonal split the opposite angles into two perfectly EQUAL halves?","options":["A square","Any rectangle, always","Only a very long, thin rectangle","This never happens in any rectangle"],"answer":0,"explain":"Because all four sides of a square are equal, its diagonal splits every angle it touches evenly."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"To find a point that is exactly 5 cm from two different fixed points B and C using only a compass, what should you do?","options":["Draw arcs of radius 5 cm centred at B and at C — where the arcs cross is the point","Draw one straight line joining B and C","Guess and check with a ruler until it looks right","It cannot be done without a protractor"],"answer":0,"explain":"Every point on an arc of radius 5 cm from a centre is exactly 5 cm away — so where two such arcs cross is 5 cm from both centres at once."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In the chapter's 'House' construction, every border line was 5 cm long, and the roof-peak point had to be exactly ___ cm from both of the wall-top points.","answer":5,"explain":"The roof-peak point was found using two compass arcs of radius 5 cm — matching the length of the house's other sides."}]},{"id":"la-boss","title":"Geometry Guardian","icon":"🏆","kind":"boss","boss":{"name":"Geometry Guardian","taunt":"Points, lines, rays, angles, circles, squares and diagonals — every shape has its secrets. Show me you know them all!"},"story":"One final test across everything in this world — points, lines, rays, angles measured in degrees, circles, squares, rectangles and their diagonals.","teach":[],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these has exactly two endpoints?","options":["A line segment","A ray","A line","None of these"],"answer":0,"explain":"A line segment is the only one of these bounded at both ends."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A right angle measures how many degrees?","answer":90,"explain":"A right angle is exactly a quarter turn, or 90°."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"An angle less than 90° is called…","options":["Acute","Obtuse","Reflex","Straight"],"answer":0,"explain":"Acute angles are sharper (smaller) than a right angle."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The vertex of an angle is…","options":["Where the two rays meet","The name of the ray","Always 90°","A type of line"],"answer":0,"explain":"The vertex is the shared starting point of the angle's two arms."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What do we call the distance from a circle's centre to its edge?","options":["Radius","Vertex","Angle","Segment"],"answer":0,"explain":"That fixed distance is the radius — it's the same to every point on the circle."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which shape's diagonal always splits its angles into two perfectly equal halves?","options":["A square","A rectangle that is not a square","Any four-sided figure","None of these"],"answer":0,"explain":"Only a square has all sides equal, which is what makes its diagonal split angles evenly."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"To find a point equidistant from two fixed points using a compass, you should draw —","options":["Arcs of equal radius centred at each of the two points","A single circle around only one point","A straight ruler line between them","Nothing — it cannot be constructed this way"],"answer":0,"explain":"Where the two equal-radius arcs cross is exactly that radius away from both centres."}]}]},{"id":"primetime6","index":3,"name":"Prime Time","subtitle":"Factors, multiples, primes and prime factorisation","emoji":"🔑","palette":{"from":"#14b8a6","to":"#0f766e"},"lessons":[{"id":"pt1","title":"Factors & Multiples","icon":"🔑","kind":"lesson","story":"Children play the 'idli-vada' game: say 'idli' for multiples of 3, 'vada' for multiples of 5, and 'idli-vada' for numbers that are multiples of both!","teach":[{"title":"Common multiples","body":"In the 'idli-vada' game, players say 'idli' for multiples of 3, 'vada' for multiples of 5, and 'idli-vada' for numbers that are multiples of BOTH 3 and 5 — like 15, 30, 45. These shared multiples are called common multiples.","visual":"🥘"},{"title":"Factors from the Jump Jackpot game","body":"In the Jump Jackpot game, Jumpy chooses a jump size and jumps from 0. If a treasure is on 24, jump sizes of 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 12 and 24 will all land on it — because these numbers all divide 24 exactly. Such numbers are called the factors (or divisors) of 24.","visual":"🦘"},{"title":"Common factors","body":"If treasures are placed on two different numbers, only jump sizes that are common factors of BOTH numbers will reach both treasures. For treasures on 14 and 36, only jump sizes 1 and 2 work — the common factors of 14 and 36.","visual":"💎"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In the 'idli-vada' game, numbers that are multiples of both 3 and 5 (like 15, 30, 45...) are called ______.","options":["common multiples of 3 and 5","prime numbers","common factors","co-primes"],"answer":0,"explain":"These numbers appear in both the multiples-of-3 list and the multiples-of-5 list."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In the Jump Jackpot game, the numbers 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 12, 24 all divide 24 exactly. These numbers are called the ______ of 24.","options":["factors (or divisors)","multiples","primes","co-primes"],"answer":0,"explain":"A factor of a number divides into it with no remainder."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"If Jumpy wants to reach both treasures placed at 14 and 36, which jump sizes work?","options":["The common factors of 14 and 36 (1 and 2)","Any jump size at all","Only jump size 1","Only jump size 14"],"answer":0,"explain":"Only jump sizes that divide both 14 and 36 exactly will land on both treasures."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A number for which the sum of all its factors equals twice the number itself is called a ______.","options":["perfect number","prime number","composite number","co-prime number"],"answer":0,"explain":"For example, 28's factors (1, 2, 4, 7, 14, 28) add up to 56, which is twice 28."}]},{"id":"pt2","title":"Prime & Composite Numbers","icon":"🔍","kind":"lesson","story":"Guna can arrange 12 figs in several rectangular ways, but Anshu can only arrange 7 figs in one way. What does this tell us about these two numbers?","teach":[{"title":"Primes and composites","body":"Numbers that have only two factors — 1 and themselves — are called prime numbers, like 2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13. Numbers with more than two factors are called composite numbers, like 4, 6, 8, 9. The number 1 is special: it has only one factor, so it is neither prime nor composite.","visual":"🔢"},{"title":"The Sieve of Eratosthenes","body":"This ancient method finds primes: cross out 1, circle 2 and cross out all its multiples, circle the next uncrossed number (3) and cross out its multiples, and keep going. All circled numbers are prime! This method was developed by the Greek mathematician Eratosthenes about 2200 years ago.","visual":"🏺"},{"title":"Twin primes","body":"Twin primes are pairs of prime numbers with a difference of exactly 2, like 3 and 5, or 17 and 19. Mathematicians still don't know if the list of twin primes ever ends!","visual":"👯"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Numbers that have exactly two factors — 1 and themselves — are called ______ numbers.","options":["prime","composite","perfect","co-prime"],"answer":0,"explain":"2, 3, 5, 7, 11 and 13 are all examples of prime numbers."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Numbers that have more than two factors are called ______ numbers.","options":["composite","prime","co-prime","perfect"],"answer":0,"explain":"4, 6, 8 and 9 are all examples of composite numbers."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Is the number 1 prime or composite?","options":["Neither — it has only one factor","Prime, since it has one factor","Composite, since it's small","Both prime and composite"],"answer":0,"explain":"1 is a special case: it does not fit the definition of prime or composite."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The ancient method of finding prime numbers by crossing out multiples of each prime, developed by a Greek mathematician, is called the ______.","options":["Sieve of Eratosthenes","Kaprekar method","Brahmagupta's method","Pythagorean sieve"],"answer":0,"explain":"Eratosthenes lived around 2200 years ago and developed this systematic method."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Twin primes are pairs of prime numbers with a difference of 2, like 3 and 5, or 17 and 19. Which of these is also a twin prime pair?","options":["29 and 31","7 and 11","13 and 19","23 and 29"],"answer":0,"explain":"29 and 31 are both prime, and differ by exactly 2."}]},{"id":"pt3","title":"Prime Factorisation","icon":"🌳","kind":"lesson","story":"Every composite number can be broken down into a unique product of prime numbers — like unlocking its most basic building blocks.","teach":[{"title":"Breaking numbers into primes","body":"Writing a number as a product of only prime numbers, like 56 = 2 × 2 × 2 × 7, is called its prime factorisation. Every number greater than 1 has exactly one prime factorisation — the primes may be written in a different order, but they are always the same set of primes.","visual":"🌳"},{"title":"Checking if numbers are co-prime","body":"Two numbers that have no common factor other than 1 are called co-prime. Using prime factorisation, we can check this by comparing the prime factors of both numbers — if they share no common prime factor, the numbers are co-prime.","visual":"🤝"},{"title":"Checking divisibility","body":"We can also use prime factorisation to check if one number divides another: if the prime factorisation of the smaller number is completely included in the prime factorisation of the larger number, then the larger number is divisible by the smaller one.","visual":"✅"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Writing a number as a product of only prime numbers, like 56 = 2 × 2 × 2 × 7, is called its ______.","options":["prime factorisation","perfect square","common multiple","digit sum"],"answer":0,"explain":"This breaks the number down into its most basic prime building blocks."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Two numbers that have no common factor other than 1 are called ______.","options":["co-prime","prime","composite","perfect"],"answer":0,"explain":"For example, 4 and 9 share no common factor besides 1, so they are co-prime."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"How can you check if two numbers are co-prime using prime factorisation?","options":["Check if they share any common prime factor — if not, they are co-prime","Add their digits together","Check if both numbers are even","Multiply them and see if the result is prime"],"answer":0,"explain":"If the two numbers share no prime factor, there is no common factor other than 1."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Every number greater than 1 has how many different prime factorisations (ignoring the order of the factors)?","options":["Exactly one","Exactly two","As many as its factors","None"],"answer":0,"explain":"This is a remarkable and important fact about prime numbers."}]},{"id":"pt-boss","title":"Prime Champion","icon":"🏆","kind":"boss","boss":{"name":"Prime Champion","taunt":"Factors, primes, and the building blocks of every number — prove you have mastered them all!"},"story":"The final challenge of Prime Time — factors, multiples, primes, composites and prime factorisation, all in one battle.","teach":[],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these numbers is prime?","options":["37","51","26","91"],"answer":0,"explain":"37 has only two factors (1 and 37); the others are all composite."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the prime factorisation of 36?","options":["2 × 2 × 3 × 3","2 × 18","4 × 9","6 × 6"],"answer":0,"explain":"36 broken down fully into primes gives two 2s and two 3s."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Are 15 and 28 co-prime?","options":["Yes — they share no common prime factor","No — they share the factor 5","No — they share the factor 2","No — they share the factor 7"],"answer":0,"explain":"15 = 3 × 5 and 28 = 2 × 2 × 7 — no prime factor is shared."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What are the only two factors of any prime number?","options":["1 and the number itself","1 and 2","The number itself and 0","2 and the number itself"],"answer":0,"explain":"This is exactly what makes a number prime."}]}]},{"id":"fractions6","index":4,"name":"Fractions Level Up","subtitle":"Unit fractions, mixed numbers, and Brahmagupta's method","emoji":"🍫","palette":{"from":"#f59e0b","to":"#b45309"},"lessons":[{"id":"fr1","title":"Unit Fractions & Mixed Numbers","icon":"🍫","kind":"lesson","story":"If one roti is shared among 2 children, or among 4 children, who gets the bigger share? Understanding fractions as equal shares is the key to everything that follows.","teach":[{"title":"Fractional units","body":"When one whole is divided into several equal parts, each part is called a fractional unit (also called a unit fraction), like 1/2, 1/3, 1/5 or 1/9. The more people sharing, the smaller each share becomes — so 1/9 is actually smaller than 1/5, even though 9 is a bigger number!","visual":"🍕"},{"title":"Fractions greater than one","body":"Fractions like 3/2, 5/2 and 7/2 are greater than 1 whole unit. We can figure out how many whole units they contain — for example, 3/2 = 1 + 1/2, meaning it contains 1 whole unit plus an extra half.","visual":"➕"},{"title":"Mixed numbers","body":"A mixed number (or mixed fraction) contains a whole number part and a fractional part less than 1, like 4½. Any fraction greater than 1 can be rewritten as a mixed number, and any mixed number can be converted back into a regular fraction.","visual":"🔢"},{"title":"Fractions on the number line","body":"Every fraction has its own spot on the number line. To place 3/5, divide the unit length between 0 and 1 into 5 equal parts and count 3 of them. There are infinitely many fractions between any two whole numbers!","visual":"📏"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"If one roti is shared among 9 children instead of 5, does each child get more or less?","options":["Less — sharing among more people gives a smaller share","More — bigger numbers always mean more","The same amount either way","It cannot be determined"],"answer":0,"explain":"This is why 1/9 is actually a smaller fraction than 1/5."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which is greater, 1/5 or 1/9?","options":["1/5","1/9","They are equal","Cannot be compared"],"answer":0,"explain":"Dividing something among fewer people (5) gives each person a bigger share than dividing it among more people (9)."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A number like 2¾, which has a whole number part and a fraction part, is called a ______.","options":["mixed number","unit fraction","denominator","improper number"],"answer":0,"explain":"Mixed numbers combine a whole part and a fractional part."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"How many whole units are there in the fraction 7/2?","answer":3,"explain":"7/2 = 3 + 1/2, so it contains 3 whole units plus a half."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"To mark 2/5 on the number line between 0 and 1, you should —","options":["Split the unit into 5 equal parts and count 2 of them","Split the unit into 2 equal parts and count 5 of them","Mark it exactly halfway between 0 and 1","Mark it at the number 2"],"answer":0,"explain":"The denominator (5) tells you how many equal parts to split the unit into; the numerator (2) tells you how many of those parts to count."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"How many different fractions lie between 0 and 1 on the number line?","options":["Infinitely many","Exactly 9","Exactly 10","None — no fractions fit between 0 and 1"],"answer":0,"explain":"You can always divide the unit into even smaller equal parts, so there is no limit to how many fractions fit between 0 and 1."}]},{"id":"fr2","title":"Adding & Subtracting Fractions","icon":"➕","kind":"lesson","story":"Brahmagupta, the great Indian mathematician, first described the general method for adding and subtracting fractions in the year 628 CE — a method we still use today!","teach":[{"title":"Same denominator, easy addition","body":"To add fractions with the same denominator, like 2/5 and 1/5, you simply add the numerators and keep the denominator the same: 2/5 + 1/5 = 3/5.","visual":"🍫"},{"title":"Equivalent fractions","body":"Fractions that represent the same amount, like 1/2, 2/4, and 4/8, are called equivalent fractions — multiplying (or dividing) both the numerator and denominator by the same number never changes a fraction's value.","visual":"🟰"},{"title":"Brahmagupta's method for different denominators","body":"To add fractions with different denominators, like 1/4 and 1/3, first convert them into equivalent fractions with the same denominator (here, 12): 1/4 = 3/12 and 1/3 = 4/12. Then add: 3/12 + 4/12 = 7/12.","visual":"🧮"},{"title":"Simplest form (lowest terms)","body":"A fraction is in its simplest form (or lowest terms) when its numerator and denominator share no common factor except 1. To simplify 16/20, divide both by their highest common factor, 4, to get 4/5 — and 4 and 5 share no common factor, so we're done.","visual":"✂️"},{"title":"Comparing fractions","body":"To compare fractions like 4/5 and 7/9, convert both to equivalent fractions with the same denominator: 4/5 = 36/45 and 7/9 = 35/45. Since 36/45 > 35/45, we know 4/5 > 7/9.","visual":"⚖️"},{"title":"A method from ancient India","body":"This method for adding and subtracting fractions was first explicitly described by the mathematician Brahmagupta in the year 628 CE, in his book the Brahmasphutasiddhanta. His method for both addition and subtraction is still exactly what we use today.","visual":"📜"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"To add 2/5 and 1/5, which have the same denominator, you simply ______.","options":["add the numerators and keep the denominator the same","add both numerators and denominators","multiply the numerators","subtract the smaller numerator"],"answer":0,"explain":"2/5 + 1/5 = 3/5, since both fractions already share the same fractional unit."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"To add fractions with different denominators, like 1/4 and 1/3, the first step of Brahmagupta's method is to ______.","options":["convert them into equivalent fractions with the same denominator","just add the numerators directly","just add the denominators directly","round both fractions to the nearest whole number"],"answer":0,"explain":"Once both fractions share a denominator, you can add their numerators directly."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Using Brahmagupta's method, what is 1/6 + 1/3?","options":["1/2","1/9","2/9","1/18"],"answer":0,"explain":"1/3 = 2/6, so 1/6 + 2/6 = 3/6, which simplifies to 1/2."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Brahmagupta's method for adding and subtracting fractions was first described in which year?","options":["628 CE","1949 CE","300 BCE","2000 CE"],"answer":0,"explain":"Brahmagupta described this method in his book, the Brahmasphutasiddhanta."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these is an equivalent fraction to 1/2?","options":["4/8","1/4","2/1","3/5"],"answer":0,"explain":"4/8 simplifies to 1/2 — multiplying numerator and denominator of 1/2 by 4 gives 4/8."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is 16/20 expressed in its simplest form (lowest terms)?","options":["4/5","8/10","2/5","16/20 is already simplest"],"answer":0,"explain":"The highest common factor of 16 and 20 is 4. Dividing both by 4 gives 4/5, which has no common factor left."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A fraction is said to be in its simplest form (lowest terms) when —","options":["Its numerator and denominator share no common factor except 1","Its numerator is 1","Its denominator is 10","It is greater than 1"],"answer":0,"explain":"Once the numerator and denominator have no common factor besides 1, the fraction can't be reduced any further."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which is greater: 4/5 or 7/9?","options":["4/5","7/9","They are equal","Cannot be compared"],"answer":0,"explain":"Converting to a common denominator of 45: 4/5 = 36/45 and 7/9 = 35/45. Since 36 > 35, 4/5 is greater."}]},{"id":"fr-boss","title":"Fraction Champion","icon":"🏆","kind":"boss","boss":{"name":"Fraction Champion","taunt":"Unit fractions, mixed numbers, and Brahmagupta's ancient method — show me you have mastered them all!"},"story":"The final challenge of Fractions Level Up — comparing, converting, and combining fractions of every kind.","teach":[],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which is greater: 4/5 or 7/9?","options":["4/5","7/9","They are equal","Cannot be compared"],"answer":0,"explain":"4/5 = 36/45 and 7/9 = 35/45, so 4/5 is slightly greater."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is 9/2 written as a mixed number?","options":["4½","3½","4¼","5"],"answer":0,"explain":"9/2 = 8/2 + 1/2 = 4 + 1/2 = 4½."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A fraction is in its 'simplest form' or 'lowest terms' when ______.","options":["its numerator and denominator have no common factor other than 1","its numerator is 1","its denominator is 10","it is a mixed number"],"answer":0,"explain":"This means the fraction cannot be reduced any further."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In ancient India, a fraction was called 'bhinna' in Sanskrit, meaning ______.","options":["broken","whole","half","big"],"answer":0,"explain":"This reflects how a fraction represents a 'broken' or divided part of a whole."}]}]},{"id":"measuresymmetry6","index":5,"name":"Area, Data & Symmetry","subtitle":"Perimeter, area, pictographs, bar graphs and symmetry","emoji":"📊","palette":{"from":"#ec4899","to":"#9d174d"},"lessons":[{"id":"ms1","title":"Perimeter & Area","icon":"📏","kind":"lesson","story":"How much tape do you need to go all around a photo frame? How much lawn is left after digging flower beds? Perimeter and area answer questions like these every day.","teach":[{"title":"Perimeter formulas","body":"The perimeter of a closed figure is the total distance around its boundary. For a rectangle: perimeter = 2 × (length + breadth). For a square: perimeter = 4 × length of a side. For a triangle: perimeter = sum of all three sides.","visual":"📐"},{"title":"Area formulas","body":"The area of a closed figure is the amount of region it encloses, measured in square units. The area of a rectangle is length × width. The area of a square is the length of one side multiplied by itself.","visual":"▦"},{"title":"Splitting shapes to find area","body":"For more complex figures, we can split them into rectangles and triangles whose areas we already know how to calculate, then add up the pieces to find the total area.","visual":"✂️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The perimeter of a rectangle is calculated as ______.","options":["2 × (length + breadth)","length × breadth","4 × length","length + breadth"],"answer":0,"explain":"This accounts for all four sides — two lengths and two breadths."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The perimeter of a square is calculated as ______.","options":["4 × length of a side","2 × length of a side","length of a side squared","length + breadth"],"answer":0,"explain":"A square has four equal sides, so multiply the side length by 4."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A square park has a side of 75 m. What is its perimeter in metres?","answer":300,"explain":"4 × 75 = 300 metres."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The area of a rectangle is calculated as ______.","options":["length × width","2 × (length + width)","length + width","4 × length"],"answer":0,"explain":"Multiplying length by width gives the total enclosed region."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A rectangular garden is 25 m long with an area of 300 sq m. What is its width, in metres?","answer":12,"explain":"300 ÷ 25 = 12 metres."}]},{"id":"ms2","title":"Data: Pictographs & Bar Graphs","icon":"📊","kind":"lesson","story":"Which game is most popular in class? How many students were absent today? Organising and presenting data helps us answer questions like these at a glance.","teach":[{"title":"Pictographs","body":"A pictograph represents data using pictures or symbols, where each picture or symbol represents a set number of items — this is called the scale or key (for example, ' = 5 students'). Pictographs let us answer questions about data with just a quick glance.","visual":"🖼️"},{"title":"Bar graphs","body":"A bar graph uses bars of uniform width, drawn horizontally or vertically with equal spacing. The length or height of each bar represents the frequency or quantity for that category, based on a chosen scale marked on the axis.","visual":"📊"},{"title":"Choosing a good scale","body":"When frequencies are large, we cannot use a scale of 1 unit = 1 item, since the graph would become too tall or wide. Instead we choose a larger scale, like 1 unit = 10 items, so the graph fits nicely and remains easy to read.","visual":"📏"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A pictograph represents data using ______.","options":["pictures or symbols, each worth a set number of items","only numbers","only bars","only lines"],"answer":0,"explain":"This makes data quick and visual to interpret at a glance."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In a pictograph, the key or scale (like ' = 5 students') tells us ______.","options":["how many items each picture or symbol represents","the total number of items","the name of the data being shown","nothing important"],"answer":0,"explain":"Without the scale, we couldn't correctly interpret how many items each picture stands for."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In a bar graph, the length or height of each bar represents ______.","options":["the frequency or quantity of that category","the width of the bar only","the colour of the bar","nothing meaningful"],"answer":0,"explain":"Longer or taller bars mean a bigger frequency or quantity for that category."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why must all the bars in a bar graph have the same width?","options":["So that only the length or height, not the width, shows the difference in values","To make the graph look colourful","Because it is a rule with no real reason","So the bars fit on the page"],"answer":0,"explain":"Equal widths make sure comparisons between bars are fair and accurate."}]},{"id":"ms3","title":"Symmetry — Reflection & Rotation","icon":"🦋","kind":"lesson","story":"A butterfly's wings, a spinning pinwheel, a rangoli pattern — symmetry is one of nature and art's most beautiful and common patterns.","teach":[{"title":"Line of symmetry","body":"A line that divides a figure into two parts which exactly overlap when folded along that line is called a line of symmetry. A figure can have more than one line of symmetry — a square, for example, has 4!","visual":"🪞"},{"title":"Reflection symmetry","body":"A figure that has one or more lines of symmetry is also said to have reflection symmetry, because each half is a mirror reflection of the other across the line.","visual":"🦋"},{"title":"Rotational symmetry","body":"A figure that looks exactly the same after being rotated by some angle less than 360° about a fixed centre point is said to have rotational symmetry. A pinwheel, for example, looks identical after every 90° turn — it has no line of symmetry, but it does have rotational symmetry!","visual":"🌀"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A line that divides a figure into two parts which exactly overlap when folded is called a ______.","options":["line of symmetry","angle of rotation","diagonal","perimeter"],"answer":0,"explain":"Folding along this line makes both halves match perfectly."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"How many lines of symmetry does a square have?","answer":4,"explain":"A square has 2 lines through the midpoints of opposite sides, plus 2 diagonal lines."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A figure that looks exactly the same after being rotated by some angle less than 360° is said to have ______.","options":["rotational symmetry","no symmetry at all","only reflection symmetry","an irregular shape"],"answer":0,"explain":"A pinwheel is a classic example — it looks the same after every 90° turn."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"True or false: every figure with rotational symmetry has 360° as one of its angles of symmetry.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"explain":"A full turn always brings any figure back to its starting position."}]},{"id":"ms-boss","title":"Measure & Symmetry Master","icon":"🏆","kind":"boss","boss":{"name":"Measure & Symmetry Master","taunt":"Perimeter, area, data and symmetry — show me you can master them all in one go!"},"story":"The final challenge of this world — perimeter, area, pictographs, bar graphs, and symmetry, all in one battle.","teach":[],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the perimeter of a rectangle with length 12 cm and breadth 8 cm?","options":["40 cm","20 cm","96 cm","44 cm"],"answer":0,"explain":"2 × (12 + 8) = 2 × 20 = 40 cm."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the area of a square with side 6 cm?","options":["36 sq cm","24 sq cm","12 sq cm","6 sq cm"],"answer":0,"explain":"6 × 6 = 36 square centimetres."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In a bar graph, if the scale is '1 unit length = 10 runs' and a bar is 8 units tall, how many runs does it represent?","options":["80","18","8","800"],"answer":0,"explain":"8 × 10 = 80 runs."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The windmill figure has rotational symmetry but no line of symmetry — what does this tell us about symmetry?","options":["A figure can have rotational symmetry without having any line of symmetry","All symmetric figures must have both kinds of symmetry","Rotational symmetry and line symmetry are the same thing","Windmills cannot be symmetric"],"answer":0,"explain":"These two types of symmetry are independent — a figure can have one, both, or neither."}]}]},{"id":"otherside6","index":6,"name":"The Other Side of Zero","subtitle":"Negative numbers, integers, and Brahmagupta's rules","emoji":"🏢","palette":{"from":"#6366f1","to":"#3730a3"},"lessons":[{"id":"os1","title":"Meeting Negative Numbers","icon":"🏢","kind":"lesson","story":"Bela's Building of Fun has floors both above AND below the ground floor — and its special lift, with '+' and '−' buttons, is about to introduce you to a whole new kind of number.","teach":[{"title":"Floors above and below zero","body":"In Bela's Building of Fun, the ground floor is Floor 0. Floors above are numbered with positive numbers (+1, +2...), reached by pressing '+'. Floors below are numbered with negative numbers (−1, −2...), reached by pressing '−'. Zero itself is neither positive nor negative.","visual":"🛗"},{"title":"Comparing floors","body":"A floor higher up represents a bigger number. Floor −4 is lower than Floor −3, so −4 < −3. All negative numbers are less than 0, and all positive numbers are greater than 0.","visual":"📶"},{"title":"What are integers?","body":"Positive numbers, negative numbers, and zero, together, are called integers. They stretch endlessly in both directions: ... −4, −3, −2, −1, 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, ...","visual":"➖➕"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In Bela's Building of Fun, floors below the ground floor (Floor 0) are numbered using ______ numbers.","options":["negative","positive","fractional","prime"],"answer":0,"explain":"You reach these floors by pressing the '−' button in the lift."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which floor number is neither positive nor negative?","options":["Floor 0","Floor +1","Floor -1","Floor +10"],"answer":0,"explain":"Zero is the special reference point that separates positive from negative."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Positive numbers, negative numbers and zero, together, are called ______.","options":["integers","fractions","primes","factors"],"answer":0,"explain":"Integers stretch endlessly in both directions from zero."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these is true?","options":["-4 < -3","-3 < -4","They are equal","Cannot be compared"],"answer":0,"explain":"Floor -4 is lower down than Floor -3, so -4 is the smaller number."}]},{"id":"os2","title":"Adding & Subtracting Integers","icon":"🛗","kind":"lesson","story":"Brahmagupta, in the year 628 CE, was the first to give clear rules for adding and subtracting positive numbers, negative numbers, and zero — rules we still use today!","teach":[{"title":"Addition as movement","body":"In the lift, Starting Floor + Movement = Target Floor. For example, starting at Floor +2 and pressing −3 takes you to Floor (+2) + (−3) = −1.","visual":"🛗"},{"title":"Brahmagupta's rules for addition","body":"The sum of two positives is positive. The sum of two negatives is negative — add the numbers and place a minus sign. To add a positive and a negative, subtract the smaller (ignoring signs) from the larger, and use the sign of the bigger number.","visual":"📜"},{"title":"Subtracting a negative is like adding","body":"Subtracting a negative number is the same as adding the corresponding positive number! For example, (+5) − (−3) = (+5) + (+3) = +8. This is one of Brahmagupta's key insights.","visual":"🔁"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"If you start at Floor +2 and press -3 in the lift, which floor do you reach?","options":["Floor -1","Floor +5","Floor -5","Floor +1"],"answer":0,"explain":"(+2) + (-3) = -1."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"According to Brahmagupta's rules, the sum of two negative numbers is ______.","options":["negative","positive","always zero","impossible to find"],"answer":0,"explain":"For example, (-2) + (-3) = -5."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Subtracting a negative number is the same as ______ the corresponding positive number.","options":["adding","subtracting","multiplying","ignoring"],"answer":0,"explain":"For example, (+5) - (-3) = (+5) + (+3) = +8."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is (+4) + (-6)?","answer":-2,"explain":"Since 6 is bigger than 4, the result takes the sign of the bigger number: -2."}]},{"id":"os-boss","title":"Integer Champion","icon":"🏆","kind":"boss","boss":{"name":"Integer Champion","taunt":"Positive, negative, and zero — prove you can handle numbers on both sides of the line!"},"story":"The final challenge of The Other Side of Zero — comparing, adding and subtracting integers of every kind.","teach":[],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which is greater: -10 or +5?","options":["+5","-10","They are equal","Cannot be compared"],"answer":0,"explain":"Every positive number is greater than every negative number."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is (-7) - (-10)?","answer":3,"explain":"Subtracting a negative is the same as adding: (-7) + (+10) = 3."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"According to Brahmagupta's rules, subtracting zero from a number gives ______.","options":["the same number","zero","the number's inverse","an undefined result"],"answer":0,"explain":"For example, -2 - 0 = -2."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"If the temperature is -2°C and it rises by 6 degrees, what is the new temperature?","options":["4°C","-8°C","8°C","-4°C"],"answer":0,"explain":"-2 + 6 = 4°C."}]}]}];